-- title:
--   Partial Atkin–Lehner automorphism of L·ℚ(X₁(Mp)), with generator law
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, $M\ge 1$ with $p\nmid M$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a $\{p\}$-cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, and let $\zeta\in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L\subseteq L((q))$ equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) of the $q$-expansion function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L137) of $\Gamma_1(Mp)$, that is, the $L$-subfield of $L((q))$ generated by the coefficientwise image [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) of that $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$; and let $j\in K$ have image [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), where $\mathtt{jq}=q^{-1}\cdot\mathtt{jNumQ}$. Then there is an $L$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $K$ with three properties. First, $\sigma j$ has image the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.qExpand ℚ p ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25), i.e. $j(q^p)$, the substitution $q\mapsto q^p$ being the exponent-scaling ring endomorphism of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$. Secondly, for every element $f$ of [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305) (the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the expansions $q\mapsto q^d$ applied to $\mathtt{jq}$, for all nonzero $d\mid Mp$) whose coefficientwise image lies in $K$, $\sigma$ sends that image to the coefficientwise image of [`ModularCurve.atkinLehnerInvolutionFull M p f`](def/ModularCurve_AtkinLehnerPartial.html#L21): the latter is a chosen $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism interchanging $\mathtt{jq}(q^d)$ and $\mathtt{jq}(q^{dp})$ for every nonzero $d\mid M$, if one exists, and the identity otherwise. Thirdly, a generator law: there are a ring embedding $\iota\colon L\to\mathbb{C}$ and $\gamma\in SL(2,\mathbb{Z})$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M)$ and $p\mid\gamma_{11}$ (the lower right entry) such that for every weight $k\in\mathbb{Z}$, all modular forms $f,g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(Mp)$, all integral power series $p_f,p_g$ with $p_f,p_g$ mapping to the $q$-expansions of $f,g$ at cusp width $1$, with [`ModularCurve.intSeriesC ℚ pg ≠ 0`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L69), and every $x\in K$ whose image is the coefficientwise image of $p_f/p_g$ in $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, there exist modular forms $\varphi_f,\varphi_g$ of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ and nonzero integers $D_f,D_g$ with $\varphi_f=D_f\cdot\bigl((f\mid_k\gamma)(\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\cdot\tau)\bigr)$ and $\varphi_g=D_g\cdot\bigl((g\mid_k\gamma)(\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\cdot\tau)\bigr)$ as functions on the upper half plane, such that each of $\varphi_f,\varphi_g$ is a finite sum $\sum_i \iota(c_i)\cdot F_i$ with $c_i\in L$ and $F_i$ forms of weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(Mp)$ admitting integral $q$-expansions, and such that in $\mathbb{C}((q))$ one has $\iota_*(\sigma x)\cdot D_f\cdot\widehat{\varphi_g}=D_g\cdot\widehat{\varphi_f}$, where $\iota_*$ is the coefficientwise map and $\widehat{\;\cdot\;}$ the $q$-expansion at width $1$.
--
--   This is the construction of the partial Atkin–Lehner automorphism $w_p$ on the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ base changed to a field containing the $p$-th roots of unity, packaged together with the defining description of $\sigma$ on ratios of integral forms (pull-back along $\gamma\cdot\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$), so that no division of $q$-series is required. It is used by the statement that additionally records how $\sigma$ conjugates the diamond operators and the coefficientwise Galois action.
